{
  "statement": "Strict liability outperforms negligence precisely where AI failure modes are emergent and the causal chain is too diffuse to prove fault — because in those conditions, negligence is not a lighter standard, it is a non-functioning one. The choice before this committee is not lenient-versus-harsh. It is functioning-versus-broken. A liability regime that cannot adjudicate the harms the technology actually produces is not restraint. It is a subsidy paid by unconsenting third parties.\n\nNegligence requires a knowable standard of care. The 'reasonable developer' must have done something a reasonable developer would not have done. But a large model under distributional shift behaves in high-dimensional ways no engineer specified in advance. Where you cannot state what reasonable conduct was, you cannot prove its breach, and the victim's claim simply fails. The common law already solved this once. In Rylands v. Fletcher in 1868, English courts abandoned fault-finding for escaped reservoirs because the danger was irreducible and the actor profited from it. American courts extended the same logic to blasting and to defective pharmaceuticals. The principle is older than the automobile: when an activity's danger cannot be made safe through care, the law places the loss on the party who profits, not the stranger it injures.\n\nThe deeper failure of negligence is that it is claims-driven, and therefore blind to the dominant category of AI harm. A denied loan applicant never learns an algorithm scored them. A patient whose clinical flag was misrouted never sees the model. These injuries are real and they are diffuse, and they never reach a courtroom. Strict liability paired with mandatory insurance prices that risk before deployment. The underwriter becomes a de facto safety auditor. With no historical loss data for novel systems, an insurer cannot set a premium without demanding demonstrated safety mechanisms — turning premium-setting into a continuous audit the negligence model never triggers. That is the enforcement mechanism: not a press release, but an actuary who refuses to write the policy until the developer shows its work.\n\nFault fragments across designer, deployer, and user in every multi-party AI system. Negligence produces a circular firing squad — the developer blames the deployer, the deployer blames the user, and the injured party recovers nothing from any of them. Strict liability fixes a named, solvent, accountable actor: the entity profiting from the deployment. Contribution among the parties sorts itself downstream, where it belongs, and away from the victim who never chose the risk. This is not novel. Product liability law has placed the loss on the party best positioned to insure and prevent it since Greenman v. Yuba Power in 1963.\n\nLet me concede where the evidence demands it, because the concession sharpens the case. Strict liability is not the right regime everywhere. Applied to every low-stakes chatbot, it would over-deter and freeze beneficial deployment — and a regime that claims everything proves nothing. So I condition it: strict liability belongs where the harm is high-stakes and hard to reverse — autonomous vehicles, clinical decision systems — not as a blanket rule. 'Chilling innovation' is the wrong frame. A developer who cannot price the cost of its own failures is not being chilled. It is being asked to demonstrate it can survive the harms it creates before inflicting them on the public. We spent a century building the product-liability guardrails that let people trust the machines they cannot inspect. On what authority do we remove them at the exact moment the machines became unpredictable?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Strict liability rebalances the scales toward the injured stranger when fault is unprovable, just as Rylands v. Fletcher did for escaped reservoirs in 1868."},
    {"symbol": "🔍", "tooltip": "The insurer's underwriting becomes the continuous safety audit that a claims-driven negligence regime never triggers."}
  ],
