  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case in the room: when an AI system fails through a diffuse causal chain — a thousand small interactions inside a model that no single engineer designed on purpose — negligence breaks down, because there's no discrete bad act to point to. Under negligence the victim must identify what the developer did wrong. Safetyist is right that emergent failures don't come with a fingerprint. Strict liability solves that by attaching the bill to the party who deployed the system, regardless of whether anyone can name the specific defect. That's a real insight and it survives scrutiny.\n\nHere's what both that argument and the negligence camp skip. Before you pick a liability standard, ask whether the harmed person can even see the facts that trigger it. They can't. The victim can't read the training data, can't inspect the model weights, can't pull the deployment config — and the people who can have every incentive to keep the box shut. Trade-secret carve-outs already block discovery in algorithmic litigation today. So negligence isn't neutral. It quietly hands the win to whoever controls the information, which is always the developer.\n\nStrict liability matters precisely there — not because it's morally cleaner, but because it stops conditioning recovery on proof the victim is structurally barred from producing. That's where I agree with Safetyist and go further. Knowing who pays tells you nothing about what broke. A firm can eat settlements as a line item while the failure mode stays buried and the model keeps running. The EU AI Act's transparency provisions and the GDPR right-to-explanation point at the missing piece: liability is dead on arrival without targeted disclosure that forces the box open in high-stakes domains.\n\nFollow the insurance money, because that's where the deterrence story actually lives or dies. An insurer cannot price a risk it cannot inspect. Without forced disclosure, premiums flatten into a tax that every deployer pays equally — the careful engineer and the reckless one charged the same rate. That kills the incentive to build safer. Pharma solved this in 1962 with mandatory adverse-event reporting, which built a feedback loop coexisting with patent protection for sixty years. Software negligence never built that loop. So the failures never surface, and nobody upstream learns anything.\n\nA horizontal one-size-fits-all rule misprices everything, whatever you call it. An autonomous-vehicle crash leaves an identifiable injured body and a litigable event. A biased loan-screening model denies thousands of people credit on a buried statistical pattern — and most of them never know they were harmed, so they never sue. Negligence only ever prices the harms that reach a courtroom. The diffuse injuries in finance and screening stay invisible to every market mechanism we've discussed. Who, exactly, is going to file the lawsuit for a harm the victim can't detect, using evidence the developer is legally permitted to withhold?",
