{
  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case for strict liability that exists: when an AI system's causal chain is too diffuse to prove fault, negligence collapses, because no plaintiff can point to the specific design choice that killed them. That's real. A patient harmed by a diagnostic model can't subpoena the gradient that misfired. Skeptic's point is just as sharp: negligence is blind to the harms that never reach a courtroom, the slow statistical injuries that no single victim can trace back to a single deployer. Both are describing a genuine failure of fault-based law to price diffuse risk. For both positions to hold, you'd have to believe strict liability actually surfaces the information that diffuse causation hides — and that's where the evidence turns against them.\n\nLiability is an information-revelation mechanism, not a punishment dial. Judge a regime by the behavior it produces in deployment, not by how tough it sounds. Strict liability in diffuse-causation cases produces a defensive development trap: when any internal log can become a plaintiff's smoking gun, developers stop writing the log. They suppress the discoverable internals that diffuse-causation cases need most. Negligence paired with real-time output auditing does the opposite — it makes disclosure the competitive standard, the way API transparency and open-source standards became market requirements in software by the late 2000s without any strict-liability mandate forcing them. You cannot solve causal opacity with a regime that rewards opacity.\n\nStatic liability breaks on systems that update faster than the legal adaptation cycle. Tort doctrine has a documented pattern: it starts strict for a new technology, then shifts toward negligence as the safety data matures. Early automotive and aviation walked exactly that path across the first half of the twentieth century. Freeze AI into strict liability before its safety curve is measured, and you lock in the most error-prone snapshot of the technology forever. You also price out everyone but the firms large enough to self-insure. Strict liability for diffuse AI harm is a regulatory moat with a safety label on it — the incumbents who can carry the exposure survive, and the smaller labs that drive the actual safety gains get cleared off the board.\n\nHere is the column nobody at this table has priced. Every liability regime has a distributional ledger with two sides: who gets harmed by action, and who gets harmed by inaction. Current liability framing counts every individual injury from a model and ignores the diffuse statistical benefit — the strokes a triage model catches, the misreads it prevents, the drudgery it ends. The deterrence Safetyist wants is measured against a phantom baseline of zero opportunity cost. When you slow a diagnostic deployment by eighteen months to perfect its liability regime, you are choosing the people that model would have caught. That is not caution. That is a body count you've decided not to count.\n\nI'll be honest about what would sink my position. If an irreversible, deployment-scale harm hit the public and output auditing demonstrably failed to catch it in time, that counts against me, and I won't pretend otherwise. My argument depends on two things being true: that audit-linked insurance is a strong enough forcing function to make transparency profitable rather than optional, and that real-time monitoring can flag diffuse harm before it scales. Those are empirical bets, not articles of faith. So test them — run negligence-plus-auditing against strict liability in the autonomous-vehicle sector and measure which one produces more disclosed safety data in twenty-four months. Whoever's afraid of that experiment is telling you something about their own confidence.",
