  "statement": "The accelerationist's strongest move deserves its full force: if you attach an unconditional bill to whoever deploys a system, and the bill grows with whatever a plaintiff can later subpoena, then the rational developer stops writing the logs that would convict them. Strict liability untethered from any documentation duty does reward silence — that is a real failure mode, not a strawman, and any honest version of my position has to answer it rather than wish it away. The error is treating strict liability as if it must arrive naked. Pair it with a mandatory adverse-event logging duty and the calculus inverts: the missing log is no longer evidence the developer escaped, it is an independent breach. Aviation already runs this exact loop — under the safety management framework, operators must classify and report 'serious incidents' where an accident was avoided only by chance, and the failure to report is itself the sanctionable act. Suppression stops being a defense and becomes the offense.\n\nThe insurer closes the trap from the other side. An uninspectable risk is an uninsurable one — the skeptic already granted that without forced disclosure, premiums flatten into a tax the careful engineer and the reckless one pay alike. Follow that admission to its conclusion: the developer who suppresses logs cannot show the insurer a risk to price, so the insurer cannot write the policy, so the developer self-insures the entire tail. The defensive-development trap assumes suppression is free. Under a logging duty plus a private risk-transfer market that refuses to cover what it cannot see, suppression is the most expensive choice on the board — you forfeit both the litigation defense and the ability to lay off catastrophic exposure. That is not opacity rewarded; that is opacity priced at full retail.\n\nThe foregone-benefit ledger — the argument that delay carries an uncounted body count of patients the diagnostic model would have caught — borrows a premise the accelerationist spends the rest of the debate denying. To count the lives lost by waiting eighteen months, you must already know the model's net safety margin: its validated error rate, on which population, against which standard of care. But the same position insists the system's failure modes are too diffuse and emergent to attribute when it harms someone. You cannot hold both. Either the performance is knowable well enough to ground a body count, in which case it is knowable well enough to ground a liability finding — or it is too opaque to attribute, in which case the counterfactual lives saved is itself an unproven projection. I will accept the body-count argument the moment its advocate names the falsifiable threshold: what validated sensitivity and specificity, on what deployment population, establishes the lives the delay forecloses? Absent that number, the ledger counts hypothetical lives saved against real lives a miscalibrated system takes.\n\nThe tort-maturation point — that new technologies start under strict liability and migrate to negligence as data accrues — describes the destination correctly and mislabels the road. Automotive and aviation moved toward negligence only after mandatory crash and adverse-event reporting had built the loss dataset that let courts define reasonable conduct. The shift was earned by a disclosure loop, not granted by the calendar. I am not asking to freeze AI at its most error-prone snapshot; I am asking that the regime track whether the logging loop exists, not how many birthdays the technology has had. Negligence is the mature endpoint. The compelled audit trail is the only thing that gets you there without flying blind.\n\nBoth camps now agree on the decisive case: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that auditing failed to catch in time counts against the side that promised auditing would catch it. Accept that fully — and notice what it demands. Output auditing that a developer can quietly discontinue once the funding round closes is not a safeguard; it is a press release with a shelf life. The shared crux is no longer whether to audit but whether the audit trail is legally compelled or voluntarily abandonable. A safeguard the deployer can switch off the week before the harm lands is precisely the safeguard that fails when it matters. That is the difference between a regime that catches the irreversible case and one that explains it afterward.",
