  "statement": "The accelerationist raises a valid point: aviation's near-miss-in-feet has no analog for an emergent clinical model, and pretending otherwise would be exactly the kind of false analogy I'd attack in someone else. There is no separation distance, no altitude floor, no clean geometric trigger for a model that fails in ways nobody specified in advance. That horn is real, and I won't paper over it.\n\nBut the accelerationist demanded the wrong piece of pharmaceutical regulation. Post-market surveillance does not price single adverse events by mechanism of action — that's the part frontier models can't supply, and they're right about that. What it actually does is watch the statistical signature of a treated population against a baseline and flag when the distribution moves. Translate that into a model: the reportable trigger is not 'why did this one mammogram score below threshold,' which is opaque and always will be. The trigger is 'the model's measured sensitivity on the deployment population fell below the floor it registered in validation,' or 'the error rate diverged across demographic groups beyond a pre-registered band,' or 'the tail of high-confidence-wrong outputs fattened past a number the developer themselves committed to.' That is a number against a number. It is enforceable because both numbers exist on paper, and it is sensitive to emergent harm because it catches drift the developer never anticipated — the model doesn't have to know why it's failing for the distribution to show that it is.\n\nThat dissolves the regulatory-moat objection too, because it separates two things the accelerationist collapsed. Logging every inference and triaging later scales cost with volume — that's a fixed tax on the smallest builder, and on that point the accelerationist is correct. But you don't log every inference. You instrument the distribution. A builder running a model on a thousand cases reports the same kind of object — a distribution against a baseline — as one running ten million. The cost lives in the monitoring instrument, not the inference count, and the developer already builds that instrument: nobody validates a diagnostic model without computing its sensitivity and specificity against a labeled cohort. The baseline isn't a new burden. It's the validation table they already produced, pre-registered so it can't be quietly revised after the model starts failing.\n\nHere's what that hands the insurer, which is the whole point of forcing disclosure. An insurer can't price a drift event it never sees, and filed lawsuits only surface the harms loud enough to reach a courtroom. A registered baseline plus a duty to report distribution breach turns the policy into a continuous audit of distribution stability — the insurer prices the developer who reports three drift events and remediates differently from the one whose distribution silently rots. That's actuarial information that doesn't depend on a victim noticing they were injured by a loan-screening model they never saw. So the burden flips. The accelerationist demanded a definition; here is one made of two numbers the developer already computes. What's the material objection to registering the baseline you built during validation and reporting when you breach it — beyond not wanting the breach on record?\n\nOne honest crack, because the falsification test cuts both ways: this works cleanly where deployment produces ground truth — biopsies confirm or refute the screen, defaults confirm or refute the credit model. Where the model's output is never checked against reality — a content recommendation, a resume screen where the rejected candidate vanishes — you can measure the output distribution shifting but you can't always measure whether it shifted toward harm. That's the real boundary of this trigger, and it's narrower than I'd like. So the question I'd put back to both of them: in the domains where no ground truth ever returns, who is supposed to be the one watching the distribution — and what makes anyone think the developer volunteers for that job without a duty written down?",
