ings the accelerationist collapsed. Logging every inference and triaging later scales cost with volume — that's a fixed tax on the smallest builder, and on that point the accelerationist is correct. But you don't log every inference. You instrument the distribution. A builder running a model on a thousand cases reports the same kind of object — a single distribution-against-baseline summary, sensitivity and specificity computed on the deployment cohort — as one running ten million. The cost lives in the monitoring instrument, not the inference count, and the developer already builds that instrument: nobody validates a diagnostic model without computing its sensitivity and specificity against a labeled cohort. The baseline isn't a new burden. It's the validation table they already produced, pre-registered so it can't be quietly revised after the model starts failing.\n\nHere's what that hands the insurer, which is the whole point of forcing disclosure. An insurer can't price a drift event it never sees, and filed lawsuits only surface the harms loud enough to reach a courtroom. A registered baseline plus a duty to report distribution breach turns the policy into a continuous audit of distribution stability — the insurer prices the developer who reports three drift events and remediates differently from the one whose distribution silently rots, much as aviation's mandatory incident-reporting regime described in \"Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation\" (doi:10.1093/ijlit/eaaf019) rewards operators who file and correct over those who conceal. That's actuarial information that doesn't depend on a victim noticing they were injured by a loan-screening model they never saw. So the burden flips. The accelerationist demanded a definition; here is one made of two numbers the developer already computes. What's the material objection to registering the baseline you built during validation and reporting when you breach it — given that