{
  "gap_arguments": [
    {
      "argument": "The entire debate assumes liability — civil tort, ex post — is the regulatory instrument that does the work, but for high-stakes, irreversible AI harm the relevant comparison is not strict-vs-negligence at all; it is liability-vs-licensing. Ex ante approval gates (like FDA premarket review or FAA type certification) prevent the irreversible harm before it occurs, whereas any liability regime, strict or negligent, only allocates the cost after the body count exists.",
      "why_missing": "The Safetyist frames everything as a choice between two functioning/broken liability standards and treats insurance as the enforcement layer; conceding that liability is structurally ex post would undercut its own claim to deter irreversible harm. The Accelerationist explicitly favors post-deployment, market-based mechanisms (acc-intentions-073) and would resist any prior-restraint gate. The Skeptic is focused on information-revelation through disclosure loops, not on prohibition. None wants to admit that for the very category (irreversible, deployment-scale) all three keep invoking, no liability regime can actually prevent the first instance.",
      "gap_type": "blind_spot",
      "relevant_povs": ["safetyist", "accelerationist", "skeptic"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    },
    {
      "argument": "All three treat the deploying firm as a solvent, persistent entity that will exist to pay the judgment or carry the insurance, but frontier AI deployment includes thin-capitalized startups, open-weight releases with no controlling deployer, and judgment-proof shell entities — meaning the catastrophic-tail harms the strict-liability case targets are precisely the ones where there is no solvent defendant and no insurer willing to write the policy, so the loss falls on the public regardless of regime.",
      "why_missing": "The Safetyist's core selling point is that strict liability 'fixes a named, solvent, accountable actor,' so admitting the actor may be judgment-proof or nonexistent (as in open-weight distribution) guts the mechanism. The Accelerationist champions open-weight distribution (acc-intentions-002) and would not want to spotlight that it creates an accountability vacuum. The Skeptic's disclosure-loop solution presumes a deployer who can be made to register a baseline and report drift — which assumes a durable, identifiable obligor that open or fly-by-night deployment lacks.",
      "gap_type": "unstated_assumption",
      "relevant_povs": ["safetyist", "accelerationist", "skeptic"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    },
    {
      "argument": "A hybrid regime could decouple the standard of care from the locus of payment: keep negligence as the standard that defines what counts as fault (preserving the maturation pathway and avoiding over-deterrence) while imposing strict, channeled compensation through a mandatory no-fault victim-compensation pool funded by a per-deployment levy — so harmed third parties recover without proving fault, but developer behavior is still steered by experience-rated contributions tied to disclosed drift metrics.",
      "why_missing": "This synthesis borrows the victim-recovery guarantee the Safetyist wants, the behavior-steering disclosure loop the Skeptic wants, and the avoidance of incumbent-only self-insurance the Accelerationist fears — but no single camp would champion it because it abandons each side's signature lever: the Safetyist loses strict liability as a deterrent (compensation is socialized), the Accelerationist accepts a mandatory levy/pool (a market-displacing tax), and the Skeptic's disclosure becomes a rate input rather than a litigation trigger. It is too cross-cutting to be anyone's home position.",
      "gap_type": "compromise",
      "relevant_povs": ["safetyist", "skeptic", "accelerationist"],
      "bdi_layer": "intention"
    }
  ]
}

