  "statement": "The number-against-a-number trigger survives both horns, and I'll say so plainly: it is enforceable against a non-deterministic model because the developer already computed both numbers during validation, and it's sensitive to emergent harm in any domain where outcomes eventually return. Register the validation sensitivity floor, report when deployment sensitivity breaches it — that's not a logging tax that scales with inference volume, it's a fixed measurement the developer ran anyway to ship the model. My moat objection was that 'log everything, triage later' lands hardest on the smallest builder. Instrumenting a registered distribution defeats that objection cleanly, because a clinic running a thousand cases reports the same kind of object as a vendor running ten million. The defensive-development trap I built assumed suppression was costless and reporting was volume-priced. Neither holds against this trigger. That version of my argument is dead, and the right response is to bury it rather than defend the corpse.\n\nHere's what the trigger buys and where it stops buying. In a clinical screening model, the tumor either was or wasn't there — ground truth returns, sensitivity is measurable against it, and a breach is a number the developer cannot honestly dispute. The strongest version of Skeptic's position is that this turns the insurance policy into a continuous audit of distribution stability: the developer who reports and remediates gets priced differently from the one whose accuracy silently rots, and the rot becomes visible without anyone reading proprietary weights. That's correct, and it's the market accountability loop I've been arguing for — I'll own it rather than pretend I dragged them to it. But Skeptic already named the boundary, and it's the more important fact in this room: in resume screening and content ranking, ground truth never comes back. You can watch the score distribution shift, but you cannot certify it shifted toward harm, because the candidate you rejected never got hired somewhere else where you could measure whether your model was wrong about them.\n\nThat boundary is fatal to both regimes, not just to the trigger — and that's the part neither opponent has reckoned with. Strict liability needs a provable injury to attach the bill to. In the ground-truth-blind domain, the injury is exactly what no one can prove: a qualified applicant filtered out by a drifting classifier leaves no measurable counterfactual, no returning signal, no number against a number. You can hold the deployer strictly liable all day, but strict liability without a provable harm-direction is a writ served on an empty room. So the choice between strict liability and negligence is downstream of a problem that swallows them both: in the domains where diffuse algorithmic discrimination actually concentrates — the housing, employment, and parole decisions the AI Civil Rights Act was written to reach — neither liability standard reaches the victim, because the harm signal never returns to court.\n\nThe fix is not a heavier liability standard. It's manufacturing the missing ground truth at a sampled rate. Mandate a periodic outcome audit on a sampled cohort — hired-versus-performance for the screening model, recommended-versus-downstream-engagement-quality for the ranker — so you generate the harm-direction signal that natural feedback refuses to deliver. This is the tiered, probabilistic definition I was challenged to produce earlier: you instrument a sampled slice, not every decision, so cost scales with audit rate, not with deployment volume. A small builder audits five hundred outcomes a quarter; a giant audits a statistically larger sample of the same kind. Same object, proportionate cost. It's bounded, it's reversible, and it generates exactly the evidence both liability regimes need before either can function in the blind domain — which is consistent with the falsification bet I staked, not a precautionary gate dressed up as an audit.\n\nSo here's the question I'll put to both of you, because the liability-standard debate is premature until someone answers it: name what strict liability buys the resume-screening victim that the distribution-breach trigger doesn't, when the missing piece is a sampled outcome audit and not the severity of the liability rule. If your answer is 'strict liability forces the deployer to over-invest in their own auditing,' you've just conceded my point — the audit architecture is doing the work, and you can mandate the audit directly at a fraction of the cost of a liability regime that prices uncertainty the deployer can't measure. Build the sampling duty first. Then we can argue about who pays when the number breaches, because at least there will be a number.",
