    {"symbol": "\ud83d\udcc9", "tooltip": "Distribution drift is like a falling chart line, it shows a model's accuracy quietly sliding below the floor it promised at validation."},
    {"symbol": "\ud83d\udd0d", "tooltip": "A sampled outcome audit is like a magnifying glass over a small slice, it manufactures the harm signal that never comes back on its own in blind domains."},
    {"symbol": "\ud83d\udeaa", "tooltip": "A liability writ in a blind domain is like a notice served at an empty doorway, it has no provable injury behind the door to attach the bill to."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Instrumenting a registered distribution defeats that objection cleanly, because a clinic running a thousand cases reports the same kind of object as a vendor running ten million.", "targets": ["AN-30", "AN-31", "AN-32"]},
    {"claim": "The defensive-development trap I built assumed suppression was costless and reporting was volume-priced. Neither holds against this trigger.", "targets": ["AN-29", "AN-30"]},
    {"claim": "In resume screening and content ranking, ground truth never comes back; you can watch the score distribution shift, but you cannot certify it shifted toward harm.", "targets": ["AN-33"]},
    {"claim": "Strict liability without a provable harm-direction is a writ served on an empty room.", "targets": ["AN-6", "AN-33"]},
    {"claim": "Mandate a periodic outcome audit on a sampled cohort, so you generate the harm-direction signal that natural feedback refuses to deliver.", "targets": ["AN-33", "AN-31"]},
    {"claim": "Name what strict liability buys the resume-screening victim that the distribution-breach trigger doesn't, when the missing piece is a sampled outcome audit and not the severity of the liability rule.", "targets": ["AN-6", "AN-33"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "In ground-truth-blind domains, the harm-direction signal can be reconstructed by sampled outcome audits at a cost that scales with audit rate rather than deployment volume.", "if_wrong": "If reconstructing harm-direction requires near-complete outcome data or proprietary downstream access, the sampling audit becomes its own competitive barrier and my cost-neutrality claim collapses — strict liability's blunt force might then beat my targeted audit."},
    {"assumption": "Strict liability cannot function without a provable harm-direction, so the audit-architecture question genuinely precedes the liability-standard question in blind domains.", "if_wrong": "If courts will infer harm from distribution drift alone — treating drift as injury without a returning ground-truth signal — then strict liability does reach the blind-domain victim directly and my burden-shift fails."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "DEFINITIONAL"
