{
  "statement": "The contradiction charge collapses once you notice it conflates two different kinds of knowing. A diagnostic model can have a sensitivity of 94 percent and a specificity of 91 percent, validated against a labeled cohort of 100,000 mammograms — that is a population-level fact you can put in a table. Knowing why one specific patient's tumor scored below threshold is a different object entirely: it requires tracing which features in that single image drove that single output, through a non-linear function nobody can read off cleanly. So here is my falsifiable threshold, since one was demanded: if a model clears 90 percent sensitivity on the deployment population in a prospective trial and the standard-of-care radiologist run rate is 85 percent, the foregone-lives figure is the delta times the screening volume during the delay — concrete, auditable, and revisable as new trial data lands. That the aggregate is knowable and the single-case attribution is opaque is not an inconsistency. It is the ordinary condition of every statistical instrument from epidemiology to actuarial tables.\n\nNow the logging duty — and I'm not going to fight the part that's right. A mandatory adverse-event reporting obligation genuinely flips the suppression math, and the pharma precedent settles it: the 1962 reporting loop ran alongside patent protection for six decades without strangling drug development. When the missing log is itself the sanctionable act, the developer who deletes it is not escaping liability, they're manufacturing a second, cleaner cause of action against themselves. Fine. That's real, and it dissolves the version of my defensive-development trap that assumed suppression was costless. So I'll take the concession and move the fight to where it actually lives.\n\nThe whole inversion rests on one undefined word: 'reportable.' Aviation's serious-incident threshold works because flight is deterministic enough to measure — a near-miss has a separation distance in feet, an altitude floor, a closure rate. You can write the trigger because the physics hands you a metric. Name the equivalent for an emergent clinical model or a screening LLM. There is no separation distance. There is no altitude floor. A 'near-miss' in a non-deterministic system is an output that almost crossed a harm boundary nobody can locate in advance. So the duty forks: either you define 'reportable' as 'any inference that could plausibly have contributed to harm,' in which case every one of millions of daily outputs is a filed event and you've built a compliance machine that buries the signal under its own volume — or you define it narrowly around known failure types, in which case the genuinely novel emergent failures, the exact harms strict liability exists to reach, fall outside the definition by construction.\n\nAnd watch who pays for the broad version. A 'log everything, triage later' regime scales its cost with inference volume, not with risk — storage, review, classification labor, all rising with throughput. A lab running ten million queries a month eats a review burden ten times the lab running one million, regardless of whether either is more dangerous. That's not a safety gradient; it's a fixed tax that lands hardest on the smallest builders, the same regulatory moat dressed in disclosure language. The insurer who declines uninspectable risk doesn't rescue this either — an insurer can only price what's been classified, and if the classification scheme is either too broad to be informative or too narrow to capture the emergent tail, the premium flattens back into the same undifferentiated levy on careful and reckless alike.\n\nSo I'll hand the bill back. The logging duty inverts my trap if and only if there exists a definition of 'reportable incident' that is simultaneously enforceable against a non-deterministic model and sensitive to emergent harm. Nobody in this debate has written that definition, because aviation's metric doesn't transfer and pharma's adverse-event categories presume a known mechanism of action that frontier models lack. Until that definition exists on paper, 'pair strict liability with a logging duty' is a promissory note — and strict liability for diffuse AI harm is riding on the note clearing. Produce the threshold that catches the novel failure without swallowing every inference, or concede that the mechanism meant to neutralize defensive development is itself the thing that triggers it.",
