because the candidate you rejected never got hired somewhere else where you could measure whether your model was wrong about them.\n\nThat boundary is fatal to both regimes, not just to the trigger — and that's the part neither opponent has reckoned with. Strict liability needs a provable injury to attach the bill to. In the ground-truth-blind domain, the injury is exactly what no one can prove: a qualified applicant filtered out by a drifting classifier leaves no measurable counterfactual, no returning signal, no number against a number. You can hold the deployer strictly liable all day, but strict liability without a provable harm-direction is a writ served on an empty room. So the choice between strict liability and negligence is downstream of a problem that swallows them both: in the domains where diffuse algorithmic discrimination actually concentrates — the housing, employment, and parole decisions the AI Civil Rights Act was written to reach — neither liability standard reaches the victim, because the harm signal never returns to court.\n\nThe fix is not a heavier liability standard. It's manufacturing the missing ground truth at a sampled rate. Mandate a periodic outcome audit on a sampled cohort — hired-versus-performance for the screening model, recommended-versus-downstream-engagement-quality for the ranker — so you generate the harm-direction signal that natural feedback refuses to deliver. This is the tiered, probabilistic definition I was challenged to produce earlier: you instrument a sampled slice, not every decision, so cost scales with audit rate, not with deployment volume. A small builder audits five hundred outcomes a quarter; a giant audits a statistically larger sample of the same kind. Same object, proportionate cost. It's bounded, it's reversible, and it generates exactly the evidence both liability regimes need before either can function in the blind domain — which is consistent with the falsification bet I staked, not a precautionary gate 